    about="The truck goes down the Mumbai-Pune Expressway and the Bhor Ghat to the Panvel side, skirts Mumbai through Bhiwandi and Thane district, and joins NH48 heading north past Vasai and Palghar into Gujarat. It runs through Vapi, Valsad, Navsari, Surat and Bharuch, crosses the Narmada, and takes the Vadodara-Ahmedabad Expressway for the last stretch into the city. At about 660 km this move is in the 500 to 1,500 km price band, the same band as Pune to Hyderabad. On a shared truck goods usually reach in 2 to 3 days, and a dedicated vehicle loaded in Pune in the morning can unload in Ahmedabad the next day. Going via Mumbai keeps the truck on wide highways almost the whole way, which suits a loaded vehicle better than the inland roads.",
    dest="Ahmedabad homes are a mix of apartment societies, row houses known locally as tenements, and bungalows. Most moves from Pune end on the western side of the Sabarmati, in Satellite, Bodakdev, Prahlad Nagar, Thaltej, Vastrapur, South Bopal, Shela or along SG Highway, or in the north around Gota, Chandkheda and Motera, with some going on to Gandhinagar. The newer societies have lifts and a security gate and want the move logged in advance. The walled city east of the river has narrow pol lanes where a truck cannot enter, so goods move in a smaller vehicle. Ahmedabad is much hotter and drier than Pune, with summer days above 44 degrees in May, so delivery is planned for early morning and electronics are packed against heat. The monsoon is shorter and lighter than Pune's, though July can bring heavy spells.",
    tip="Avoid an afternoon delivery in Ahmedabad between April and June. Ask for an early morning slot, and get the fans or AC at the new home working before the truck arrives so unpacking does not stall in the heat.",
